The key insight is that two numbers can be traversed between if they share a common prime factor (since gcd > 1 means they share at least one prime factor). 

We use Union-Find to group indices by their prime factors. For each number, we find its prime factors. For each prime factor, we union the current index with any previously seen index that also had this prime factor. This effectively connects all numbers sharing a common prime into one component.

After processing all numbers, we check if all indices belong to the same connected component. If yes, then any pair of indices can be traversed; otherwise, not.

Special case: if any number is 1 and there's more than one number, it's impossible because gcd(1, x) = 1 for any x > 1, so 1 can't connect to anything. This is handled early.

The time complexity is O(N * sqrt(M)) where M is the maximum value in nums (up to 10^5), which is efficient enough for the given constraints. The space complexity is O(N + M) for the Union-Find structure and the prime-to-index mapping.

